                %li
                  %a{:href => "#SimplicialAndDerived"} Simplicial and derived adjunctions
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#localizations"} Localizations
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The notion of adjunction between two
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functors"} (∞,1)-functors
            generalizes the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functors"} adjoint functors
            from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category theory
          %p
            There are many equivalent definitions of the ordinary notion of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor"} adjoint functor
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
            than others.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                One definition of ordinary adjoint functors says that a pair of functors
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mover
                        %munder
                          %mo →
                          %mi R
                        %mover
                          %mo ←
                          %mi L
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \stackrel{\overset{L}{\leftarrow}}{\underset{R}{\to}} D
                is an adjunction if there is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural isomorphism
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Hom
                        %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi L
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≃
                      %msub
                        %mi Hom
                        %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo ,
                      %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      Hom_C(L(-),-) \simeq Hom_D(-,R(-))
                      \\,.
              %p
                The analog of this definition makes sense very generally in
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+theory"} (∞,1)-category theory
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Hom
                        %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mi op
                      %mo ×
                      %mi C
                      %mo →
                      %mn ∞
                      %mi Grpd
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom_C(-,-) : C^{op} \times C \to \infty Grpd
                is the
                = succeed "-categorical" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn ∞
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %li
              %p
                One other characterization of adjoint functors in terms of their
                = succeed "/" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cograph+of+a+functor"} cographs
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heteromorphisms"} heteromorphisms
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+fibrations"} Cartesian fibrations
                to which the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/(infinity%2C1)-Grothendieck+construction#FibsOverInterval"} functor is associated
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cograph+of+a+functor"} cograph of a functor
                it is discussed how two functors
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                      %mo :
                      %mi C
                      %mo →
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L : C \to D
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                      %mo :
                      %mi D
                      %mo →
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R : D \to C
                are adjoint precisely if the cograph of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
                coincides with the cograph of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                up to the obvious reversal of arrows
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi L
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⇔
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi cograph
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi cograph
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi R
                    %mi op
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi op
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (L \dashv R) \Leftrightarrow
                  (cograph(L) \simeq cograph(R^{op})^{op})
                  \\,.
          %p
            Using the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-Grothendieck+construction"} (∞,1)-Grothendieck construction
            the notion of cograph of a functor has an evident generalization to
            = succeed "-categories." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
          %h2#definition Definition
          %h3#CharacterizationInTermsOfHomEquivalences In terms of hom-equivalences
          #InTermsOfHomEquivalences.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              %strong (in terms of hom equivalence induced by unit map)
            %p
              A pair of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functors"} (∞,1)-functors
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mover
                      %munder
                        %mo →
                        %mi R
                      %mover
                        %mo ←
                        %mi L
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    C \stackrel{\overset{L}{\leftarrow}}{\underset{R}{\to}}
                    D
            %p
              is an adjunction, if there exists a
              %em unit transformation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi Id
                      %mi D
                    %mo →
                    %mi R
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon : Id_D \to R \circ L
              – a morphism in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+of+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functors"} (∞,1)-category of (∞,1)-functors
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Func
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi D
                    %mo ,
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Func(D,D)
              – such that for all
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d \in D
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \in C
              the induced morphism
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Hom
                      %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi d
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi R
                          %mrow
                            %mi L
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi d
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ,
                            %mi c
                    %msub
                      %mi Hom
                      %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi d
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Hom
                          %mi D
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi ϵ
                        %mo ,
                        %mi R
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi c
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mi Hom
                      %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi d
                    %mo ,
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Hom_C(L(d),c)
                    \\stackrel{R_{L(d), c}}{\to}
                    Hom_D(R(L(d)), R(c))
                    \\stackrel{Hom_D(\epsilon, R(c))}{\to}
                    Hom_D(d,R(c))
            %p
              is an
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} equivalence of ∞-groupoids
          %p
            In terms of the concrete incarnation of the notion of
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-category"} quasi-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Hom
                    %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom_C(L(d),c)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Hom
                    %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mo ,
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom_D(d,R(c))
            are incarnated as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-object+in+a+quasi-category"} hom-objects in quasi-categories
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complexes"} Kan complexes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+equivalence"} homotopy equivalence
            of Kan complexes.
          %p
            In this form is due to
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Lurie09"} Lurie 09, Def. 5.2.2.7
          %p#RiehlVerityOnAdjunctionsViaHomEquivalences
            Streamlined discussion is in
            %a{:href => "#RiehlVerity15"} Riehl &amp; Verity 15, 4.4.2-4.4.4
            and
            %a{:href => "#RiehlVerity20"} Riehl &amp; Verity 20, 3.3.3-3.5.1
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#RVElements"} Riehl &amp; Verity “Elements”, Prop. 4.1.1
          %h3#InTermsOfCographsHeteromorphisms In terms of cographs/heteromorphisms
          %p
            We discuss here the quasi-category theoretic analog of
            %em
              %a{:href => "cograph+of+a+functor#AdjointFunctorsInTermsOfCographs"} Adjoint functors in terms of cographs
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heteromorphisms"}> heteromorphisms
            ).
          %p
            We make use here of the explicit realization of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-Grothendieck+construction"} (∞,1)-Grothendieck construction
            in its incarnation for
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-categories"} quasi-categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functors"} (∞,1)-functors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mo :
                  %mi D
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L : D \to C
            may be regarded as a map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta[1]^{op} \to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29Cat"} (∞,1)Cat
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+fibration"} Cartesian fibration
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi coGraph
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} coGraph(L) \to \Delta[1]
          #InTermsOfCoCartesianFibrations.num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              %strong (in terms of Cartesian/coCartesian fibrations)
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              be
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-categories"} quasi-categories
              %strong adjunction
              between
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              is
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  a morphism
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi K
                        %mo →
                        %mi Δ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K \to \Delta[1]
                  of
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+fibration"} Cartesian fibration
                  as well as a coCartesian fibration.
              %li
                %p
                  together with
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+quasi-categories"} equivalence of quasi-categories
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                        %mover
                          %mo →
                          %mo ≃
                        %msub
                          %mi K
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                            %mn 0
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \stackrel{\simeq}{\to} K_{\{0\}}
                  and
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi D
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mo ≃
                          %msub
                            %mi K
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                              %mn 1
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D \stackrel{\simeq}{\to} K_{\{1\}}
            %p
              Two
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functors"} (∞,1)-functors
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L : C \to D
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo :
                    %mi D
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R : D \to C
              are called
              %strong adjoint
              – with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              %em left adjoint
              to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              %em right adjoint
              to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              if
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  there exists an adjunction
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi K
                        %mo →
                        %mi I
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K \to I
                  in the above sense
              %li
                %p
                  and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi L
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
                  and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi K
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
                  are the
                  %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/(infinity%2C1)-Grothendieck+construction#FibsOverInterval"} associated functors to
                  the Cartesian fibation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi K
                        %mo →
                        %mi Δ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \colon K \to \Delta[1]
                  and the Cartesian fibration
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi p
                            %mi op
                          %mo :
                          %msup
                            %mi K
                            %mi op
                          %mo →
                          %mi Δ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %mn 1
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                            %mi op
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p^{op} : K^{op} \to \Delta[1]^{op}
          %h3#InTheHomotopy2Category In the homotopy 2-category
          #InTermsOfTheHomotopy2Category.num_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              %strong (in terms of the homotopy 2-category)
            %p
              Say that a 2-categorical pair of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functors"} adjoint (∞,1)-functors
              is an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
              in the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+2-category+of+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} homotopy 2-category of (∞,1)-categories
          %p
            This concept, in the spirit of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+%28infinity%2C1%29-category+theory"}
                formal
                = succeed "-category" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %a{:href => "#Joyal08"} Joyal 2008, p. 159 (11 of 348)
            and then expanded on in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#RiehlVerity15"} Riehl-Verity 15, Def. 4.0.1
          %p
            Such a 2-categorical adjunctions (Def.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#InTermsOfTheHomotopy2Category"}
            = succeed "-functors" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %a{:href => "#Lurie"} Lurie 2009
            (
            %a{:href => "#RiehlVerity15"}> Riehl-Verity 15, Rem. 4.4.5
            ):
          %p
          #InfinityCollageTypeAdjunctionsAreAdjunctionsIn2Ho.num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              An anti-parallal pair of morphisms in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29Cat"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Cat
                        %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat_\infty
              is a pair of adjoint
              = succeed "-functors" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              %a{:href => "#Lurie"} Lurie 2009, Sec. 5.2
              if and only its image in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+2-category"} homotopy 2-category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+2-category+of+%28infinity%2C1%29-categories"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Ho
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi Cat
                        %mn ∞
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ho_2\big(Cat_\infty\big)
              forms an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
              in the classical sense of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category+theory"} 2-category theory
              (Def.
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#InTermsOfTheHomotopy2Category"}
          (
          %a{:href => "#RVElements"}> Riehl &amp; Verity 2022, Sec. F.5, Prop. F.5.6
          )
          %p
            The conceptual content of Prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#InfinityCollageTypeAdjunctionsAreAdjunctionsIn2Ho"}
            may be made manifest as follows:
          .num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              Every 2-categorical pair of adjoint
              = succeed "-functors" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#InTermsOfTheHomotopy2Category"}
              extends to a “homotopy coherent adjunction” in an essentially unique way.
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#RiehlVerity16"}> Riehl &amp; Verity 2016, Thm. 4.3.11, 4.4.11
            )
          %h2#properties Properties
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-categories"} quasi-categories
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  in terms of Hom-equivalence induced by unit maps (Def.
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#InTermsOfHomEquivalences"}
              %li
                %p
                  in terms of Cartesian/coCartesian fibrations (Def.
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#InTermsOfCoCartesianFibrations"}
            %p are equivalent.
          %p
            This is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Topos+Theory"} HTT, prop 5.2.2.8
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              First we discuss how to produce the unit for an adjunction from the data of a correspondence
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo →
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K \to \Delta[1]
              that encodes an
              = succeed "-adjunction" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo ⊣
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f \dashv g)
            %p
              For that, define a morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo ′
                    %mo :
                    %mi Λ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 2
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ×
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F' : \Lambda[2]_2 \times C \to K
              as follows:
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  on
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mn 0
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{0,2\}
                  it is the morphism
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
